Based on my analysis, this section adds a `QuerySet.explain()` method to the Django ORM core. This is a framework-level database introspection feature (like `exists()`, `count()`, `first()`), not an application-level endpoint. The authorization model in Django is that the ORM layer does not perform per-request authorization — that is the responsibility of the application/view layer. The `explain()` method simply executes an `EXPLAIN` SQL statement on the same query the caller already has access to construct. There is no user-controlled identifier being fetched without ownership verification (CWE-639), no protected operation exposed without authentication at the framework level (CWE-862), and no incorrect authorization check (CWE-863). The policy documents I found describe application-level authorization patterns (ModelAdmin permissions, auth backends) but do not impose authorization requirements on ORM query methods themselves.

